    Aggregate OOS Sharpe: 0.38

    5yr/2yr (4 windows):
    Sharpe range: +0.20 to +1.12   ← much tighter, all positive
    Aggregate OOS Sharpe: 0.22

    Aggregate OOS Sharpe dropped (your prediction was right), but per-window variance actually decreased with larger windows. 
    The reason: each 2-year test window spans more market regimes — it averages across bull and bear periods — so extreme values like +2.94 or -1.17 get smoothed out. 
    Fewer windows also means one bad period has an outsized effect on the aggregate.

    Trade Off:
    Small windows → more windows → more data points → better statistics
               → but each window is regime-specific → high variance

    Large windows → fewer windows → fewer data points → weaker statistics
                → but each window covers more regimes → lower variance

    In practice, 3yr train / 1yr test is the most common configuration for daily strategies. It balances sample size against regime diversity.

